  "title": "Spin sectors of the knot's count",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "Let H be a three-fold space with n points, B its incidence graph, embedded by a minimal map of genus g, and L a contact (line). For each of the 4^g spin structures q (quadratic forms on H_1 of the surface) put N_q(B) = (3/2)^n sum (-1)^q([chi]) (i/sqrt 3)^|chi| over the even subgraphs chi of B, and N_q(B - L) = 2 (3/2)^(n-1) times the same sum over even subgraphs of B - L. (1) Spin sum: #col(H) = 2^-g sum_q (-1)^Arf(q) N_q(B) and #col(H - L) = 2^-g sum_q (-1)^Arf(q) N_q(B - L) = 2 A(H, L); so the knot's non-colourability is the balance of its even and odd spin sectors, sum over even q of N_q(B) = sum over odd q. (2) Of the 4^g Kasteleyn sign classes of a minimal map, the knot's own is the only one that is not 2-colourable: every nonzero cohomology twist is 2-colourable. (3) On the Fano torus the sectors are 8, 8, 8 (even) and 24 (odd) for the closed space and 16, 16, 16 and 28 with one contact opened, on all 16 maps and all 7 contacts; the even sectors are the knot's own three spin structures; so the amplitude 5 = (3 x 16 - 28)/4 is not carried by the even spin structures, and the odd sector does not cancel. (4) On the 13-point space (genus 2) the 4 x 4 matrices of sector values indexed by the spin structures of the two handles have rank 4, closed and opened: the sectors do not factor over handles, though the amplitude does (25 = 5 x 5).",
